    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Considerable attention has been devoted to factors affecting the persistence of women and historically underrepresented ethnic groups in their science education trajectories. The literature has focused more on structural factors that affect longitudinal outcomes rather than classroom experiences. This exploratory survey study examined relationships among high school chemistry students’ perceptions of a constructivist learning environment (CLE) and STEM career expectations.  The  sample  included  693  students  from  7  public  high  schools  within  the San Francisco Bay Area. Students’ perceptions of a CLE predicted their expectations of entering a science career, but not engineering, computer, health, or mathematics-related careers. When all groups of students perceived the learning environment as more constructivist, they were more likely to expect science careers.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
